    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nThe firing angle control range for a single-phase half-wave rectifier with a resistive load and a counter emf is determined by the point at which the supply voltage equals the counter emf. Given the peak source voltage (V_peak = 400 V) and the load counter emf (E_load = 200 V), the firing angle (α) can be found using the relationship V_peak = E_load * (1 + cos(α)). Solving for α gives us α = arccos((V_peak/E_load) - 1) = arccos((400/200) - 1) = arccos(1) = 0°. However, since the counter emf is 200V, which is half the peak voltage, the range of firing angles will be from when the supply voltage just exceeds the counter emf (approximately 60°) to the end of the positive half cycle (180°). The correct range is thus from 60° to 150° because the actual firing angle will never reach 180° due to the presence of the counter emf. Option C (60° to 150°) is the closest to this range.",
