    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10927",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A television picture is composed of approximately 300,000 individual picture elements, each of which can attain ten different and distinguishable brightness levels (such as black and shades of gray) for proper contrast. If, for any picture element, the probability of occurrence of the ten brightness levels are equal, and 30 picture frames are being transmitted per second, determine the bandwidth of the T.V. video signal. Assume that the signal-to-noise ratio of 1000 (30 db) is required for proper reproduction of any picture.\n\nOptions: \nA. 2.5MHz\nB. 1.5MHz\nC. 6MHz\nD. 8MHz\nE. 2MHz\nF. 4MHz\nG. 3MHz\nH. 1MHz\nI. 7MHz\nJ. 5MHz\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nThe bandwidth required for a television signal can be estimated using the formula for the channel capacity (Shannon-Hartley theorem), which states that channel capacity C is equal to the bandwidth B multiplied by the logarithm base 2 of (1 + signal-to-noise ratio SNR). Given the SNR is 1000 (30 dB), the channel capacity can be estimated as B * log2(1001) ≈ B * 9.97, because log2(1001) is approximately 9.97. Since each picture element can have 10 brightness levels, each requires about 3.32 bits to encode (as log2(10) ≈ 3.32). With 300,000 picture elements and 30 frames per second, the data rate is approximately 300,000 * 3.32 * 30 bits per second. Dividing this by the number of bits that can be transmitted per Hz (which is the channel capacity), we get the required bandwidth, which is close to 6MHz. Therefore, the closest answer from the given options is 6MHz (C).",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 233,
        "output_tokens": 235,
        "total_tokens": 468
