    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10034",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A Gaussian filter with \\vertH(\\omega)\\vert^2 = e^-(\\omega)2 is used to filter noise at the output of an amplifier stage. Determine the bandwidth of the filter.\n\nOptions: \nA. (1 / 3\\surd\\pi)\nB. (3 / 4√π)\nC. (1 / 6√π)\nD. (2 / √π)\nE. (1 / 4\\surd\\pi)\nF. (1 / 2\\surd\\pi)\nG. (1 / 8√π)\nH. (1 / √π)\nI. (1 / 5\\surd\\pi)\nJ. (1 / 7√π)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{H}\n\nThe bandwidth of a Gaussian filter is typically defined as the frequency at which the magnitude of the filter's frequency response function drops to 1/e (approximately 36.8%) of its DC value. For a Gaussian filter characterized by \\(|H(\\omega)|^2 = e^{-(\\omega^2)}\\), the magnitude \\(|H(\\omega)|\\) is \\(e^{-\\omega^2/2}\\). The bandwidth corresponds to when \\(|H(\\omega)| = e^{-1}\\). Given \\(|H(\\omega)| = e^{-\\omega^2/2}\\), setting this equal to \\(e^{-1}\\) and solving for \\(\\omega\\) gives \\(\\omega = 1\\). The bandwidth is often taken as the distance from the center frequency to the point where \\(|H(\\omega)|\\) is \\(e^{-1}\\), which for a Gaussian filter is \\(1/\\sqrt{2}\\). However, the given options do not conform to this standard approach. Instead, the options appear to represent a normalization factor related to the Gaussian function. The correct choice is the one that corresponds to the normalized bandwidth for a Gaussian function, which is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}\\). Thus, the correct answer is H.",
