    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The transfer function of an RC low-pass filter is given as H(f) = [1 / {{1 +jf/f_c}}] wheref_cis the 3-dB frequency. If the input to this filter is a white noise, find the output noise power N_0 of the filter.\n\nOptions: \nA. \\((\\eta f_c\\pi) / 8\\)\nB. \\(5(\\eta f_c\\pi) / 2\\)\nC. (\\etaf_c\\pi) / 3\nD. \\(\\eta f_c\\)\nE. (\\etaf_c\\pi) / 2\nF. (\\etaf_c\\pi) / 4\nG. \\(3(\\eta f_c\\pi) / 4\\)\nH. \\(4(\\eta f_c\\pi)\\)\nI. 2(\\etaf_c\\pi)\nJ. \\(\\eta f_c / \\pi\\)\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The output noise power \\(N_0\\) of an RC low-pass filter with a white noise input can be calculated by considering that the power density of white noise is constant across all frequencies and that the filter reduces the power density by the square of the magnitude of its transfer function \\(|H(f)|^2\\). At the 3-dB frequency \\(f_c\\), \\(|H(f_c)|^2 = 1/2\\). Therefore, the output noise power \\(N_0\\) is given by \\(N_0 = N_i \\cdot |H(f_c)|^2\\), where \\(N_i\\) is the input noise power. Since the input noise power spectral density is constant, we can express \\(N_i\\) as \\(\\eta f_c\\), where \\(\\eta\\) is the noise spectral density. Plugging in the values, we get \\(N_0 = (\\eta f_c) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\eta f_c}{2}\\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{E}\\)",
