      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}\n\nThe electrostatic energy (U) of a system of charges can be calculated using the formula \\( U = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_k Q_k V_k \\), where \\( Q_k \\) is the charge and \\( V_k \\) is the potential due to all other charges. For a point charge q at the center of a spherical shell, the potential inside the shell (at radius \\( r_1 \\)) is \\( V = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r_1} \\), and outside the shell (at radius \\( r_2 \\)), it is \\( V = \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r_2} \\). The energy is thus \\( \\frac{1}{2} q \\left( \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r_1} - \\frac{q}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0 r_2} \\right) \\), which simplifies to \\( \\frac{1}{8\\pi\\epsilon_0} q^2 \\left( \\frac{1}{r_1} - \\frac{1}{r_2} \\right) \\), equivalent to option D.",
