    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\nThe kilovolt-ampere rating of an autotransformer is typically higher than that of a two-winding transformer because only a portion of the winding carries the full load current. However, the exact kVA rating depends on the turns ratio. Since the original transformer is a 10 kVA unit with a turns ratio of 440/110, the autotransformer with a turns ratio of 550/440 will have a kVA rating of approximately 11 kVA (10 kVA times the square of the turns ratio increase). However, the question asks for the rating as a 550/440-volt autotransformer, which likely implies we consider only the load on the lower voltage side, effectively making it around 5.5 kVA, corresponding to option B. For the efficiency at 80% lagging power factor, since core losses remain constant and copper losses increase with load and current, the efficiency will be lower than the efficiency at unity power factor, but the closest match to the given choices is option B with 95.5%.",
