    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{E}\n\nThe RMS (Root Mean Square) value of a sine wave is given by \\( I_{RMS} = \\frac{I_{peak}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\). Given that \\( I_{RMS} \\) is 30 amps, the peak current \\( I_{peak} \\) is \\( 30 \\times \\sqrt{2} \\approx 42.4 \\) amps. The frequency \\( f \\) of the wave can be found by \\( f = \\frac{1}{T} \\), where \\( T \\) is the period, which is \\( \\frac{1}{25} \\) seconds or 0.04 seconds, giving a frequency of 25 Hz. The angular frequency \\( \\omega \\) is \\( 2\\pi f \\), which is approximately \\( 2\\pi \\times 25 \\) or 157 rad/s. The given equation for the sine wave should be \\( i = I_{peak} \\sin(\\omega t) \\) or \\( i = I_{peak} \\cos(\\omega t) \\), which leads to \\( i = 42.4 \\sin(157t) \\) or \\( i = 42.4 \\cos(157t) \\). At \\( t = 0.005 \\) seconds, the fraction of the cycle is \\( \\frac{0.005}{0.04} = \\frac{1}{8} \\) of a cycle. The cosine function is chosen because the wave crosses the time axis positively at \\( t = 0 \\), which corresponds to the cosine function's starting point. Therefore, the correct answer is E.",
