      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{D}\n\nWhen two AaBbCc individuals mate, each allele has a 1/2 chance of being passed on to the offspring. For each gene (A, B, C), the chances of the offspring receiving the same allele from both parents (homozygous for that allele) is 1/4 (1/2 chance from each parent). Since there are three genes, the probability of being homozygous for all three (AABBCC) is (1/4) * (1/4) * (1/4) = 1/64. However, since each gene can independently result in a homozygous dominant or homozygous recessive offspring, the correct probability is actually double that, or 1/32.",
