    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4590",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"With 78 percent of the Union electorate casting ballots, Lincoln was reelected in an Electoral College landslide, 212 to McClellan's 21. The 55% popular vote for the president was the third largest in the nineteenth century, surpassed only by Jackson's first victory in 1828 and Grant's reelection in 1872. McClellan won only New Jersey, Delaware, and Kentucky. Republicans drew support from native-born farmers, skilled and professional workers, those of New England descent, younger voters, and military personnel. Democrats were strongest in the cities and among Irish- and German-Americans (the most populous immigrant groups). It has been estimated that Lincoln received 78% of the vote of Union soldiers and sailors. The figure was not necessary for his reelection, but was perhaps the margin of victory in a few close states and, more importantly, of great symbolic value. Republicans also gained seats in Congress to retain unassailable control, 149 to 42 in the House and 42 to 10 in the Senate; took back several state legislatures; and lost only the governorship of New Jersey (McClellan's home state).\"\nElection of 1864, Harpweek.com\nWhich of the following conclusions is best supported by the excerpt above?\n\nOptions: \nA. Republicans managed to gain control of Congress through the efforts of Lincoln to campaign on their behalf.\nB. Lincoln won the Election of 1864 due to Union successes during the Civil War.\nC. McClellan's home state, New Jersey, was the only state where he had significant support.\nD. The number of votes cast in the Election of 1864 was one of the lowest in the nineteenth century.\nE. The majority of the votes for Lincoln were from the younger demographic.\nF. The majority of Lincoln's votes came from military personnel.\nG. The Democrats' stronghold was in the rural areas and among farmers.\nH. Lincoln received more votes in the heavily populated states of the North, while McClellan won more sparsely populated states.\nI. The Emancipation Proclamation mobilized black voters in the South to vote for Lincoln in the Election of 1864.\nJ. Lincoln's victory was primarily due to the support of the immigrant population.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "glm-4-air",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\nThe excerpt does not explicitly state that Lincoln won due to Union successes during the Civil War, but the context implies that Lincoln's reelection was bolstered by the Union electorate's support, which could be linked to military successes. The information that Lincoln received a large portion of the Union soldiers' and sailors' votes and that Republicans gained seats in Congress suggests broad support possibly related to the war effort. None of the other options are as well supported by the information provided in the excerpt.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
